  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a 2-bit binary counter work?",
      "a": "Two flip-flops count clock edges. Q0 toggles on every edge and Q1 toggles whenever Q0 falls from 1 to 0, so together they step through 00, 01, 10, 11 and roll over to 00.",
      "asks": [
        "How do flip-flops count?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a counter divide frequency?",
      "a": "Each toggling flip-flop changes state once per input cycle, so its output completes one cycle for every two input cycles. A chain of n flip-flops divides the frequency by 2 to the n.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a frequency divider?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a modulus counter?",
      "a": "The modulus is the number of states a counter goes through before repeating. n flip-flops give up to 2 to the n states, and extra logic can reset the count early, as in a mod-10 decade counter.",
      "asks": [
        "What does mod-4 counter mean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a shift register used for?",
      "a": "It moves bits along a chain of flip-flops, one place per clock edge. It converts serial data to parallel and back, delays signals, and drives long rows of LEDs from a few pins.",
      "asks": [
        "What does a shift register do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is serial in, parallel out?",
      "a": "Bits enter one at a time on a single wire, and after as many clock edges as there are flip-flops, all of them can be read together on separate outputs. It is how serial links rebuild bytes.",
      "asks": [
        "What is SIPO?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "A quartz watch crystal runs at 32,768 Hz, which is 2¹⁵, so 15 halvings give exactly 1 Hz.",
    "The 74HC595 is a widely used 8-bit serial-in, parallel-out shift register.",
    "An n-bit binary counter has 2ⁿ states, from 0 to 2ⁿ − 1."
